  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1044 PM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nFog trends through late this week...More dense fog is possible\nlate tonight into Wednesday morning. Return SW flow is kicking in\njust off surface, compared to light E-SE flow last night. Still\nwith lingering moisture and conditions saturating late tonight,\ncould see patchy to areas of dense fog. Latest trends not as \npessimistic with visibility perhaps due to the less favorable flow\noff the deck, so do not plan to issue Dense Fog Advisory yet, but\nwill have to watch this overnight. Areas most favored for dense\nfog would be near the lakeshore and inland to the west/northwest \nof the Fox Valley. High pressure remains overhead until late this \nweek. A weak boundary moving through tonight will switch winds \nback to NE direction late tonight into Thursday morning, which \ncould lead to slightly better setup for fog with added light flow \noff bay and Lake Michigan. Still an onshore wind and cooling late\nThursday night and Friday morning as well, so that spells more \nfog. Pattern breaks down Friday night with strengthening SW winds \nand warmer nighttime temps.\n\nDry through Saturday, then at least small chances for showers...\nBrush of a northern stream wave is too moisture starved to produce\nany rain late today into this evening. Main impact will be the\nsurface trough/wind shift dropping through. Omega block pattern \nwill then remain in place the rest of this week. Stronger \nshortwave trough shifts across central Canada Saturday, but most \nof the lift with this remains over northern Manitoba and Ontario \nCanada. Increasing PWATs (toward 90th percentile) pooling along \nfront tied to the trough may bring a few showers (20-30% chances) \nespecially to northern WI Saturday night. May see an isolated \nshower over rest of the area on Sunday morning, but chances no \nmore than 10-25%. Chances of some showers on Monday seem to be \ntrending higher as weekend frontal boundary settles across and \nthere are signs of tighter low-mid level thermal gradient \n(frontogenesis) in right entrance region of jet over the upper \nGreat Lakes. Based on probabilities of exceeding 0.10 inch, rain \nlooks pretty light at this point, maybe heading toward a high \nPOP/low QPF type setup. Deterministic GFS and ECMWF and associated\nMOS guidance supporting LREF idea right now as well, and latest\nNBM is trending higher as well with 20-40% chances, so confidence\nis increasing for at least some lighter showers around.\n\nCooler early next week: Post-frontal cold advection will usher in\na cooler and more seasonable airmass for Monday and Tuesday as \nsurface high pressure builds south from Manitoba. This could\nreally be the case Monday if more clouds and some rain are present\nto the north of cold front. Overall daytime highs early next week\nwill be in the upper 40s to mid 50s, coolest over the northwoods.\nLow temperatures will drop back into the low to mid 30s as the \nbuilding high promotes ideal radiational cooling at night.",
